    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d110">

                <pb facs="0177" n="165" sameAs="frus:frus1946v07/pg165"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00119 EW/5–1846: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Chargé in Greece</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Rankin</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">confidential</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1946-05-22T20:00:00-04:00">May 22, 1946—8
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>645. Urtel 665, May 18.<anchor n="12"
                        xml:id="d110-f1"/> Greeks were told by Dept several months ago that loans
                    could not be made against reparations.<anchor n="13" xml:id="d110-f2"/> In
                    connection rumors of request for 200 million dollar Eximbank loan you should take every opportunity informally to call
                    attention of Greek Govt officials to fact that none of 25 million dollar Eximbank loan has been utilized by Greece
                    during more than four months since it was granted Jan 12.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Byrnes</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d110-f1" n="12" xml:id="d110-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed; it summarized a <hi rendition="#italic">note verbale</hi> of May 10
                    from the Greek Foreign Office which outlined the “alleged dire economic plight”
                    of Greece because of astronomic war losses and expressed the conviction that
                    Greece should be accorded priority rights to reparations payments similar to
                    those granted to Belgium after World War (740.0011EW/5–1846).</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d110-f2" n="13" xml:id="d110-f2-content">
                <p>For a quotation from the relevant memorandum of January 11, 1946, to the Greek
                    Embassy, see footnote 43a, <ref target="frus1945v08#pg_284">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, 1945, vol. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">viii</hi>, p. 284</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
